  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="stack" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.1 · vocabulary</span>
        <h2>Six layers, six different jobs.</h2>
        <p>The names are often collapsed into “Wasm”. Dekopon uses each layer for a distinct reason.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="definition-stack">
        <article><span>artifact</span><h3>Core WebAssembly module</h3><p>Low-level instructions, functions, tables, and linear memory. Rust’s <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> target emits this layer without WASI.</p><code>.wasm</code></article>
        <article><span>composition</span><h3>WebAssembly component</h3><p>A typed wrapper around one or more core modules. It exposes component-model interfaces rather than only core numeric signatures.</p><code>component</code></article>
        <article><span>interface</span><h3>WIT</h3><p>WebAssembly Interface Types describes worlds, interfaces, imports, exports, records, variants, resources, strings, and lists. It is language-neutral source-level IDL.</p><code>world provider</code></article>
        <article><span>generated glue</span><h3><code>wit-bindgen</code> + Wasmtime bindgen</h3><p>The guest SDK generates export adapters; the host generates typed call handles. They agree because both consume the mirrored WIT world.</p><code>Guest ↔ Provider</code></article>
        <article><span>runtime</span><h3>Wasmtime</h3><p>Validates and compiles the component, owns the engine and stores, performs canonical ABI lifting/lowering, and executes guest machine code.</p><code>Engine / Store · 36.0.12</code></article>
        <article><span>capability wiring</span><h3>Linker</h3><p>Matches every component import to a host implementation. What the host does not define cannot be called. Exports are looked up after instantiation.</p><code>Linker&lt;T&gt;</code></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-plain">
        <strong>The shortest useful distinction</strong>
        <p><b>WIT says what may be called.</b> Generated bindings say how each language calls it. The canonical ABI says how values are represented at the core boundary. Wasmtime performs the call. The linker decides which imported powers exist.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="build" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.2 · build pipeline</span>
        <h2>From ordinary Rust to a typed component.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="build-pipeline">
        <div class="build-step"><span>1</span><strong>Provider Rust</strong><small><code>impl Provider</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>2</span><strong>Guest adapter</strong><small><code>export_provider!</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>3</span><strong>Core module</strong><small><code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>4</span><strong>Componentize</strong><small><code>wasm-tools component new</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step build-step-final"><span>5</span><strong>Provider component</strong><small>typed exports · zero imports</small></div>
      </div>

      <div class="code-pair">
        <div class="almanac-code-block">
          <div class="code-block-label"><span>provider.wit</span><a href="https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/blob/{{ almanac.source.revision }}/crates/dekopon-provider-sdk/wit/provider.wit">source ↗</a></div>
          <pre><code><span class="code-purple">package</span> dekopon:provider@0.1.0;

<span class="code-purple">world</span> provider {
    <span class="code-green">export</span> describe: func() -&gt; string;
    <span class="code-green">export</span> invoke: func(
        capability: string,
        input-json: string,
    ) -&gt; string;
}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="almanac-code-block">
          <div class="code-block-label"><span>echo/src/lib.rs</span><a href="https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/blob/{{ almanac.source.revision }}/examples/providers/echo/src/lib.rs">source ↗</a></div>
          <pre><code><span class="code-purple">impl</span> Provider <span class="code-purple">for</span> EchoProvider {
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> manifest() -&gt; ProviderManifest { … }

    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> invoke(
        capability: &amp;CapabilityId,
        input: Value,
    ) -&gt; Result&lt;Value, ProviderError&gt; { … }
}

<span class="code-green">export_provider!</span>(EchoProvider);</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>The macro does not expose the trait directly. It generates a WIT-facing adapter that accepts strings, parses identifiers and JSON, calls the typed Rust implementation, and serializes a tagged <code>ComponentResponse</code>. Componentization packages that adapter and its WIT metadata into the artifact Wasmtime loads.</p>

      <div class="fact-row">
        <div><span>target</span><strong><code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code></strong><p>No operating-system contract is assumed.</p></div>
        <div><span>WIT package</span><strong><code>dekopon:provider@0.1.0</code></strong><p>The same import-free world is published for toolchains.</p></div>
        <div><span>not accepted</span><strong><code>wasm32-wasip2</code></strong><p>That build imports WASI, which the current linker does not supply.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="directions" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.3 · call direction</span>
        <h2>“Linking Rust to Wasm” can mean opposite things.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="direction-diagrams">
        <article>
          <div>{{ status("current", "Current") }}</div>
          <h3>Host Rust calls a guest export</h3>
          <div class="direction-flow">
            <span><small>host Rust</small><b><code>call_invoke</code></b></span>
            <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
            <span><small>canonical ABI</small><b>lift / lower</b></span>
            <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
            <span><small>guest Rust</small><b><code>Provider::invoke</code></b></span>
          </div>
          <p>The component exports <code>invoke</code>. Wasmtime’s generated host binding finds that export after instantiation and calls it. No host function is added to the linker for this direction.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <div>{{ status("direction", "Future mechanism") }}</div>
          <h3>Guest Wasm calls a Rust host import</h3>
          <div class="direction-flow direction-flow-reverse">
            <span><small>guest Wasm</small><b><code>http.request</code></b></span>
            <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
            <span><small>component import</small><b>WIT contract</b></span>
            <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
            <span><small>host Rust</small><b><code>func_wrap_async</code></b></span>
          </div>
          <p>The WIT world must import an interface and the host must register a matching implementation in the linker. The future interface name and shape are not yet designed; the diagram shows the mechanism, not a promised API.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>An export is functionality. An import is requested authority.</strong>
        <p>A component can offer pure computation through exports without receiving any host power. Filesystem, network, clock, random, secrets, and provider APIs become reachable only when both the component requests them and the host links them.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

