    <section id="stores" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.5 · object lifetimes</span>
        <h2>Compile once; isolate each call.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="lifetime-board">
        <div class="lifetime-long">
          <span>ProviderRegistry lifetime</span>
          <div><strong>Engine</strong><small>shared compiler/runtime configuration</small></div>
          <div><strong>Component</strong><small>compiled machine code retained in memory</small></div>
          <div><strong>route map</strong><small>capability ID → provider index</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="lifetime-calls">
          <span>Every <code>describe</code> or <code>invoke</code></span>
          <div><b>call 1</b><i>fresh Store</i><i>fresh instance</i><i>fresh linear memory</i></div>
          <div><b>call 2</b><i>fresh Store</i><i>fresh instance</i><i>fresh linear memory</i></div>
          <div><b>call n</b><i>fresh Store</i><i>fresh instance</i><i>fresh linear memory</i></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>A <code>Store&lt;StoreState&gt;</code> owns invocation state and resource limits. It is not a process and it is not an OS thread. In current immediate mode, the main thread creates the Store and synchronously enters guest code. A runtime mutex serializes all component execution. A short-lived deadline helper thread waits and increments the engine epoch only if the call exceeds its wall-clock limit.</p>

      <div class="current-facts">
        <article><span>shared</span><h3>Engine + compiled components</h3><p>Shared only inside one registry and one process. There is no on-disk or cross-process compilation cache.</p></article>
        <article><span>fresh</span><h3>Store + instance</h3><p>Memory, fuel, deadline, and guest state do not carry from one description or invocation to the next.</p></article>
        <article><span>serialized</span><h3>Execution lock</h3><p>Even if callers appeared concurrently, one shared mutex currently admits only one component operation at a time.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="authority" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.6 · security consequence</span>
        <h2>The empty linker is executable least privilege.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="authority-equation">
        <span>component imports</span><b>∩</b><span>host definitions</span><b>=</b><strong>guest-reachable host authority</strong>
      </div>
      <p>For the current provider world, the left side is empty. A malicious provider can loop, allocate within limits, transform its input, trap, or return malicious output—but it cannot name a linked operation that opens a file or socket, reads a clock, obtains random bytes, looks up an environment variable, or fetches a credential.</p>
      <p>That boundary is strong but narrow. It does not make all Wasm safe, defend a compromised host, validate arbitrary provider semantics, or authorize an external effect. Future imports must be derived from a broker-owned authorization and constrained more narrowly than “give this component network”.</p>

